1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,780
Pareto efficient, if and only if.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:04,200
As you guys are familiar with IFF.

3
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:09,080
Yes, so a word that's, you know, if you have no background

4
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:10,520
in math, you may think it's a typo, but no.

5
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:11,580
It means if and only if.

6
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:13,700
So it's Pareto efficient, if and only if.

7
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:15,800
The only way to make one person better off is

8
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:17,200
to make another person worse off.

9
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:19,580
The only way to make one person better off is

10
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:20,780
to make another person worse off.

11
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:22,980
And then there is a related concept,

12
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:24,640
the concept of the Pareto improvement.

13
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:26,920
A Pareto improvement is any change

14
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:27,880
that makes someone better off

15
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:29,220
without making anyone else worse off.

16
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:32,280
So notice, the Pareto efficiency is an evaluation

17
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:34,480
of states of affairs.

18
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:37,940
Pareto improvement is an evaluation of changes.

19
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:39,560
Evaluation of changes.

20
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:44,280
Right. Now a slight variation that you may have heard is a

21
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:46,020
situation that's Pareto efficient, if and only

22
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:47,640
if there's no way to make everyone better off.

23
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:50,020
Now in English there's a huge difference

24
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:50,940
between these two things.

25
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:53,520
Wait. So, wait, is the same thing

26
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:55,800
to make one person better off and make no one else worse off

27
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:57,680
or to make everyone better off?

28
00:00:57,680 --> 00:00:59,740
How are those two things the same?

29
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:03,980
One is making one person out of 7 billion better off

30
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:06,500
and the other 7 billion minus one people the same.

31
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:09,240
And another one is making all 7 billion people better off.

32
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:13,560
But the answer is that normally this concept is used

33
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:16,900
in a model of perfect mathematical continuity.

34
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:22,000
And in such a model, then one follows very naturally

35
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,860
from the other because if you manage to squeeze

36
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:27,260
out one penny's worth of gain for one person

37
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:30,540
in a continuous world, then how could you benefit everybody?

38
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:33,900
Just give everybody 1 7 billionth of that penny

39
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:37,300
and then everybody has a very tiny gain.

40
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:38,520
All right.

41
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:43,660
So, now in theory it is possible

42
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:45,780
that someone could propose a genuine,

43
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:50,380
a genuine bona fide Pareto improvement and a bunch

44
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:51,200
of people will complain.

45
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:53,660
It's possible in fact that I could propose a Pareto

46
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:55,700
improvement and everyone will complain and say

47
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:57,440
that my idea is the worst idea they ever heard

48
00:01:57,760 --> 00:01:58,680
and will destroy the world.

49
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:00,360
That is possible.

50
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:01,540
All right.

51
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:04,500
Why? Well, for strategic reasons.

52
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:08,020
So, suppose that I'm offering you a job

53
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:09,780
as a system professor of economics

54
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:11,520
and I say how does $200,000 sound?

55
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:13,040
All right.

56
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:14,660
Now, one thing you might say, you know,

57
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:15,780
$2,000, $100,000 a year.

58
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,000
You might just say sold, awesome.

59
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:19,540
On the other hand, you might say,

60
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:21,360
man this guy is really dumb.

61
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,900
He doesn't have any idea what the market is like.

62
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:28,220
So, you might say $200,000, how would I survive on that

63
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,020
and start complaining about it.

64
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:33,500
I mean, I hear that like everybody gets that at least.

65
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:35,360
I mean, you could just start complaining

66
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:38,480
about my offer despite the fact that it's a great deal for you.

67
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:40,000
Now, do not try this at home.

68
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:44,540
But, I'm sure you have noticed times when you propose a deal

69
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:45,880
that is really good for somebody

70
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:47,300
and they start complaining about it.

71
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,320
So, maybe they're just confused

72
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:51,480
but another possibility is they're very clever

73
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:54,080
and they sense in your generous offer weakness.

74
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:56,860
And, from that weakness they hope to profit

75
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:58,020
by asking for even more.

76
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:00,920
This is really blatant if you try offering kids a deal

77
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:03,120
and say, hey kids, how would you like to have three chocolates?

78
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:05,660
So, I'm only three, how can you give me three?

79
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:06,480
And, they start crying.

80
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:08,820
You're like, man, before I said anything you had no chocolates.

81
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:12,100
I just offered you three and now you're crying at me.

82
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:14,560
So, but they're trying to get more.

83
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:16,460
I'm going to give you the chocolate.

84
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:17,140
All right.

85
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:21,580
So, and sometimes with certain kinds of weak parents this works.

86
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:22,580
And, they start crying.

87
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,740
You offer them a favor and then they complain

88
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:25,820
and then they get even more.

89
00:03:25,820 --> 00:03:26,900
All right.

90
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:31,340
So, what this means is that there's a big difference

91
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:34,500
between a standard of a Pareto improvement

92
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:37,020
and what could be called a demonstrative preference

93
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:40,220
standard where everyone says, yes, I agree, this is fantastic.

94
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:41,740
All right.

95
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:44,080
So, if you are fond of Austrian economics,

96
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:45,040
sometimes they will talk

97
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:47,320
about the demonstrative preference standard.

98
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,300
The Pareto standard is different from that

99
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:51,020
because it admits the possibility

100
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:54,100
that you could deliver a Pareto improvement

101
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:57,100
that is universally denounced and everyone says, no way,

102
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:59,700
I hate your idea and I hate you.

103
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:02,580
And, I absolutely refuse to go along with it.

104
00:04:02,940 --> 00:04:05,540
And, then you do it and everyone may be quietly thinking, wow,

105
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:07,000
this is way better than what I had before.

106
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:08,020
All right.

107
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:08,760
So, that's possible.

108
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:11,120
Does this mean that it's always that way?

109
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:11,840
No, of course not.

110
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:15,800
But, you know, the Pareto improvement standard is not

111
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:18,900
in any way the same as everyone vocally agrees

112
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:19,940
that they like what you're offering.

113
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,500
And, it's possible in theory.

114
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:25,620
And, you have probably observed plenty of times in practice

115
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,260
where someone has actually bought an idea

116
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:30,360
that would be an improvement for them.

117
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:31,520
Why do they do that?

118
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:32,900
Because they want to, because they,

119
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:34,540
is it because they don't like improvements?

120
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:35,920
No, it's because they want more.

121
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:38,720
And, if you offer them something, then they sense

122
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:40,260
that maybe there's even more where that came from.

123
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:40,940
Yes.

124
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:43,980
I, from what I understand from demonstrated preference is

125
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:46,460
that it's the thing that people actually, in fact,

126
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:49,060
would choose if given the option, not what they say.

127
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:52,220
So, someone who says I would buy magazines

128
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:55,180
with normal looking women in them, but when they actually go

129
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:56,760
and buy magazines, they buy ones with.

130
00:04:56,760 --> 00:05:01,460
So, that, so, like, the answer again is there is a multiplicity

131
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:02,740
of the language that people use.

132
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:04,740
But, among Austrian economists

133
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:06,420
who like the demonstrated preference standard,

134
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:09,860
really the goal, really the only way you can actually truly

135
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:11,980
say that something does, does improve people

136
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:13,120
in this demonstrated preference standard is

137
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:14,180
if they in fact make the trade.

138
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:18,380
So, if you go and pass the hat for,

139
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:20,780
to raise $100,000 for asteroid deflection

140
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:23,720
and everyone, otherwise everyone dies and you don't manage

141
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:26,500
to raise any money, this would actually by the standard show

142
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:29,020
that people do not in fact prefer the donation

143
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:31,820
to getting destroyed, even though that may seem strange.

144
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:33,500
But, yes.

145
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:35,300
So, and again, sometimes, well,

146
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:36,900
it's just that they haven't proven that they,

147
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:38,920
that they actually would rather donate

148
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:40,420
or that they prefer the money to,

149
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:42,660
or they prefer not being destroyed to donating money.

150
00:05:42,660 --> 00:05:42,980
But, yes.

151
00:05:42,980 --> 00:05:46,260
There is this other point of there's a difference

152
00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:47,300
between what people say

153
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:48,900
and what actually makes them better off.

154
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:52,700
And, when we get to psychology and economics, we'll talk a bit

155
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:56,260
about what psychologists call social desirability bias,

156
00:05:56,260 --> 00:05:58,300
tendency of people to say what sounds good rather

157
00:05:58,300 --> 00:06:01,900
than what's true, which is a very interesting field

158
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:03,900
of research because it is the empirical proof

159
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:04,940
of what economists have been saying

160
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:06,340
without any evidence for all this time.

161
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:10,780
Namely, that people often actually say one thing

162
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:12,020
but want another thing.

163
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:14,220
I can see most of the economists I talk to, it's just like,

164
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:15,620
well, people could be lying.

165
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:17,420
I suppose they could be, but do you have any actual evidence

166
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:18,220
for that?

167
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:19,420
Prove that they're not lying.

168
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:20,820
Sounds kind of paranoid.

169
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:24,300
But, psychologists actually do try

170
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:27,060
to document real empirical differences

171
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:31,100
between what people say and what people in fact do when they are

172
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:33,540
in that situation, such as which magazines they're going

173
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:35,500
to buy, which magazines they're going to watch.

174
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:38,140
Which magazines they're going to buy.

175
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:39,140
All right.

176
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:42,780
Now, in a highly stylized theoretical setting, right,

177
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:45,340
and what is a highly stylized theoretical setting?

178
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:49,080
It's a homework problem or a model in a journal.

179
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:50,380
All right.

180
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:52,380
That's what we mean by a highly stylized theoretical setting.

181
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:54,400
It's one where we construct it perfectly

182
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:57,600
so that all the pieces fit nicely, right.

183
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:01,140
In these settings, Pareto improvements are conceivable.

184
00:07:01,140 --> 00:07:02,440
So, in settings like this,

185
00:07:02,440 --> 00:07:04,260
Pareto improvements are conceivable.

186
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:06,460
For example, if we have a world

187
00:07:06,460 --> 00:07:09,180
where everyone has identical preferences and endowments

188
00:07:09,180 --> 00:07:11,640
and there's an externality.

189
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:11,840
All right.

190
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:14,180
So, how do we get that?

191
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:15,940
Well, you know, you just think about homework problems,

192
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:16,220
you're done.

193
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:17,940
Everyone has identical preferences.

194
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:19,980
Everyone has identical endowments.

195
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:23,020
There's a negative externality of pollution.

196
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:24,320
Everyone's polluting.

197
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:27,340
And what would be a, what could be a Pareto improvement

198
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:29,300
if we just point a gun at everybody's head and say,

199
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:33,340
hey everybody, pollute 5% less now.

200
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:34,740
All right. And in this world,

201
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:38,060
it is possible that's a Pareto improvement.

202
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:43,580
Like so. And that's the whole set up of a model like that.

203
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:44,820
So, that is possible.

204
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:46,660
And you might say that people don't like being coerced.

205
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:47,740
Well, they don't like being coerced,

206
00:07:47,740 --> 00:07:50,060
but they also do like sometimes.

207
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:53,100
And in fact, in this model, they definitely like what happens

208
00:07:53,100 --> 00:07:55,420
when other people besides themselves are coerced.

209
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:59,660
And we can set the model up very easily so that the dislike you

210
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:02,180
experience of having the gun at your head is much,

211
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:05,220
is smaller than the like that you get for the benefits

212
00:08:05,220 --> 00:08:07,820
of the gun being pointed at the heads of everybody but yourself

213
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:10,380
for a net positive of the gun being pointed

214
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:11,980
at everybody's head.

215
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:12,140
All right.

216
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:16,180
So, that is totally doable in a model.

217
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:17,460
All right.

218
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:21,660
Okay. But, but, there's a very strong argument

219
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:23,500
in the real world, right.

220
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:26,060
The real world is different from a homework problem.

221
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:27,660
The real world is different from a model

222
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:29,420
that you publish in a journal.

223
00:08:29,420 --> 00:08:32,020
The homework problem or the model, you can tailor it

224
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:36,460
to make it work to be just the way you want to prove your point.

225
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:38,740
But, the real world, you don't get to do that.

226
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:40,500
Right. The real world, you don't get

227
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:43,540
to actually make everything work just so to make it perfect.

228
00:08:43,540 --> 00:08:44,700
At least, usually you don't.

229
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:46,180
I never have.

230
00:08:46,180 --> 00:08:48,660
I've never been able to make the world perfect so far.

231
00:08:48,660 --> 00:08:50,620
I don't know if anyone else had this opportunity.

232
00:08:50,620 --> 00:08:51,900
Like, this is my day.

233
00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:54,740
I'm doing everything right.

234
00:08:54,740 --> 00:08:55,060
All right.

235
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:57,820
So, in the real world, there's a strong argument

236
00:08:57,820 --> 00:09:00,460
that two weird things are true.

237
00:09:00,460 --> 00:09:01,180
All right.

238
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:05,820
So, weird thing number one, everything's Pareto efficient.

239
00:09:05,820 --> 00:09:06,620
All right.

240
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:13,140
So, that's weird thing number one.

241
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:16,380
And, weird thing number two is Pareto improvements

242
00:09:16,380 --> 00:09:18,060
are impossible.

243
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:18,780
All right.

244
00:09:18,780 --> 00:09:24,700
Now, I should warn you.

245
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:27,300
These are two things that are almost never mentioned

246
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:29,340
in this class, in a way when people teach this class

247
00:09:29,340 --> 00:09:30,740
around the world.

248
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:33,260
And, yet, they are almost certainly true,

249
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:37,300
as I think I can convince you if you think about the real world.

250
00:09:37,300 --> 00:09:37,540
All right.

251
00:09:37,540 --> 00:09:39,700
So, why would these things be true?

252
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:44,980
Simple. It's highly unlikely that any change will literally

253
00:09:44,980 --> 00:09:46,340
hurt no one.

254
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:50,060
Any change that you can imagine is going to have some person

255
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:53,260
who loses at least a small amount.

256
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:55,860
Furthermore, while many economists wave their hands

257
00:09:55,860 --> 00:09:57,740
and say, well, we just compensate the losers.

258
00:09:57,740 --> 00:10:00,980
In the real world, compensating losers is really hard.

259
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:04,180
And, remember, if it's to be a genuine Pareto improvement,

260
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:05,980
what percent of the losers do we have

261
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:07,900
to actually successfully compensate?

262
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:09,580
One hundred percent.

263
00:10:09,580 --> 00:10:11,860
We can't miss a single loser.

264
00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:14,780
Furthermore, even if we compensate every loser,

265
